{"name":"Feather Gold","hex":"#edd382","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feather","wikidata":"Q81025"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Parts of a feather: VaneShaft, rachisBarbsAftershaft, afterfeatherQuill, calamus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParts_of_feather_modified.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior region of a contour feather from a rock dove, visualized using scanning electron microscopy. Interlocking barbules are clearly seen in the middle image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APigeon_down_SEM.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia